WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.550
Hey guys, welcome back to my series called&nbsp;
Introducing. Today is a special episode. Today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:11.790
I'm leaving my comfort zone&nbsp;
of Western Classical music&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:16.290
and we are heading east. Today we're&nbsp;
going to learn about the Arabic oud.

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:45.360
So I am fascinated by the oud for a number&nbsp;
of reasons but mainly because the oud is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:53.940
ancestor of the European lute. That European&nbsp;
lute by the 15th century would fully blossom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:59.310
and become one of the most important instruments&nbsp;
of the musical period we call the Renaissance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:12.330
So in 711 the Moors, who were the inhabitants&nbsp;
of northwest Africa of the time, they decide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:18.990
to invade Spain(The Iberian Peninsula) and when&nbsp;
they did they brought with them the oud. In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:25.710
Arabic world the oud was commonly referred to as&nbsp;
the king or the Sultan of all instruments and in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:32.130
fact the "oud" or "al'oud" in Arabic simply means&nbsp;
"from wood." So, you know, it's an instrument&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:37.710
made of wood. The oud is a likely descendant&nbsp;
of an ancient instrument called the Barbat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:45.390
which was a Persian instrument dating back to&nbsp;
something like 3500 years ago. Now the earliest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:53.400
form of the oud would have just four strings made&nbsp;
of gut, sheep intestine. And over the centuries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.400 --> 00:02:00.210
they added more and more strings until nowadays&nbsp;
the common stringing for an oud is 11 strings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:08.700
That's five doubled strings tuned in unison's and&nbsp;
then one low string, which is single. So for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:20.220
tuning, we have C, two F's, two A's, two D's,&nbsp;
two g's, and two C's. So notice all the strings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:28.200
are tuned in perfect fourths except for the F to&nbsp;
the A, which is a major third. So this instrument&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:35.190
is played with a plectrum or a pick and this&nbsp;
is called a "Risha" which just means feather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:41.460
and that's because the original plectrums for this&nbsp;
instrument would have likely been an Eagles quill.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:51.490
But today often players just use plastic or wood&nbsp;
like a piece of bamboo. All ouds are pear-shaped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:59.140
on the soundboard and they typically have three&nbsp;
sound holes. Sometimes they are full of incredibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:06.370
beautiful ornate decorations. The back of the&nbsp;
instruments is always bowl shaped and this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:12.280
made up of individual strips of wood called ribs&nbsp;
usually made of a hardwood which are heated up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:17.710
and bent and glued together to make this gorgeous&nbsp;
shape here, which also gives the instrument its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.710 --> 00:03:25.720
characteristic tone. Then on top here we have a&nbsp;
bent backward peg box, which is quite beautiful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:33.430
And we have friction pegs just like a violin these&nbsp;
pegs you just push in and turn and that's what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:39.520
holds the notes at pitch. The most interesting&nbsp;
and distinguishing part of this instrument for me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:46.720
as a fretted player is that this doesn't have any&nbsp;
frets. This is a fretless instrument. Now at first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:52.480
it seems a bit daunting not to have frets however&nbsp;
it has its advantages. For example, I can slide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:59.080
between notes right I have no frets in the way.&nbsp;
So I have infinite gradations between each note&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:05.950
and when I play a melody instead of playing three&nbsp;
distinct pitches all the time, I can for example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:14.930
slide between them, which very characteristic&nbsp;
of that sound. In the beginning of this video I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.930 --> 00:04:29.630
played a Turkish song called you Uskudara Giderken&nbsp;
and the simple melody just sounds like this but I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:36.920
was utilizing a lot of those ornamental slides&nbsp;
to make the song sound more vocal and in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:41.810
oud players are often meant to improvise over&nbsp;
the melodies to make them more interesting. So I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:57.590
came up with... Another reason this instrument is&nbsp;
fretless is in order to play all of the varieties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.590 --> 00:05:07.310
of maqams. A maqam is simply a scale. There are&nbsp;
roughly something like a hundred regularly used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:13.610
maqams in Arabic music and when you compare&nbsp;
that to basically the seven church modes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:17.930
we use in western classical music, maybe plus&nbsp;
the chromatic scale in the whole tone scale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:25.070
it's pretty impressive. Okay, so let's hear&nbsp;
some of these maqams. The easiest one for me to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.070 --> 00:05:30.980
wrap my head around is called Nahawand and that's&nbsp;
basically the Aeolian mode which is just the minor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:40.040
scale. So in D, it would be D, E, F, A, Bb, C, D.&nbsp;
And that's actually the same scale I was using for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:48.080
the Turkish tune Uskudara Giderken. Another maqam&nbsp;
that I found pretty relatable is called Kurd and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:54.140
that's basically the Phrygian mode. So it's the&nbsp;
same as the natural minor scale the nahawand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.140 --> 00:06:13.610
maqam, except there's an E flat which makes it&nbsp;
Phrygian. So we have D, Eb, F, G, A, Bb, C, D.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.610 --> 00:06:20.990
Now here's where things get complicated,&nbsp;
especially for me as a complete newbie to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.990 --> 00:06:26.870
the oud and Arabic music in general. So depending&nbsp;
on which become you're using you might have to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:34.730
microtones. Microtones that exist in between the&nbsp;
western semitone. The western scale is basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.730 --> 00:06:42.620
an octave. So C to C divided up into 12 half steps&nbsp;
or semitones. Each semitone is like one key on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:51.530
piano or one fret on the guitar but in Arabic&nbsp;
music certain Maqams use notes in between those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.530 --> 00:07:03.710
Western semitones. So for example if we take A and&nbsp;
then we use a Bb. That's about a Western semitone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.710 --> 00:07:14.030
A B half-flat would be sharp. So something around&nbsp;
there and apparently depending on which region&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:21.260
you're from, that'll determine just how sharp or&nbsp;
flat that semitone is and I find that to be just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:27.800
so cool. It's so challenging to my ear because it&nbsp;
just sounds out of tune because I'm only used to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.800 --> 00:07:33.530
playing in the western parameters but if we hear&nbsp;
it in context we can start to understand why that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.530 --> 00:07:43.610
can add some interesting flavor to the music. So&nbsp;
here's the normal b-flat and now the behalf flat.

00:07:45.940 --> 00:07:52.990
It actually makes a lot of sense to me now when I&nbsp;
hear it in context. It doesn't sound out of tune,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.990 --> 00:07:59.500
it just sounds kind of like a different emotional&nbsp;
effect. My favorite Maqam that I found so far is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:09.100
called Hijaz and basically that is D, E half-flat,&nbsp;
somewhere around there so it's like a sharp E&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:28.180
flat, F#, G, A, B half- flat, C, D. When I first&nbsp;
heard that scale, I thought it kind of reminded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.180 --> 00:08:44.730
me of that theme from pulp fiction. If I decide&nbsp;
to take this instrument seriously and study it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.730 --> 00:08:50.700
one of the challenges would be memorizing a&nbsp;
long list of maqams and then being able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:58.620
endlessly improvise over any of them. This is&nbsp;
another beautiful piece called Halfouine. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:44.930
uses the nahawand maqam which again is basically&nbsp;
Aeolian. So bringing us into the 21st century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:51.560
the oud is no longer just an Arabic instrument.&nbsp;
It comes in many shapes and sizes and is played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:58.010
all around the world in many different musical&nbsp;
styles. It's such a beautiful and versatile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.010 --> 00:10:05.900
instrument and if you are as fascinated about&nbsp;
this instrument and its music as I am, I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.900 --> 00:10:12.620
strongly suggest that you listen to some real oud&nbsp;
players. I am just a beginner on this instrument.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.620 --> 00:10:19.970
I have officially taken one oud lesson but I have&nbsp;
just scratched the surface of the capabilities of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.970 --> 00:10:25.670
this instrument. If you want to hear what this&nbsp;
instrument can really do, I suggest you listen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.670 --> 00:10:31.700
to some of the great oud players of the world.&nbsp;
My favorite right now is a Tunisian player named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.700 --> 00:10:41.900
Anouar Brahem. There's also this fantastic Iraqi&nbsp;
oud player named Naseer Shamma. Alright guys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.900 --> 00:10:47.180
I hope you enjoyed learning a bit about the&nbsp;
oud today with me. If you haven't already,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.180 --> 00:10:52.940
please do SUBSCRIBE to this channel and if you hit&nbsp;
the bell notification, you'll get an email when I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.940 --> 00:10:59.060
release my next video of my introducing series. So&nbsp;
thanks for watching and we'll see you next time!

